urce name and exactly two scopes, publish and settings, so a connection approved without the settings tick can publish pages and cannot touch branding, custom domains, members or billing. It earns the guide on the stable link and what surrounds it: republishing a slug puts a new version behind the same link so a client's bookmark does not rot and rename_page keeps the short link while redirecting the old addresses; set_access carries team, public, password, people (emails and @domains) or inherit with password and people-only pages needing Pro and pages in a board following the board; list_versions and restore_version give history and rollback; get_page_views reports how often a page was opened and by whom on a people-only page; and a per-page JSON store read with docpin.data() is updated by set_page_data without republishing, which is what a dashboard actually needs because layout and numbers become separate artifacts. The write envelope is recorded the way the read envelope is: page data capped at 1 MB and about 30 writes a minute per workspace with 413 and 429 rather than silent truncation, create_upload_link taking a file up to 25 MB through a one-time link valid 15 minutes, a draft returning url null with a previewUrl good for about an hour and explicitly not for sharing, and set_draft to share requiring an access value so the agent has to ask who should be allowed in rather than defaulting to public. Free is 3 pages; Pro at 8 dollars a month adds unlimited pages, custom domains, password and people-only pages and a smaller badge. The page runs in a sandboxed frame under docpinusercontent.com, one address per published version, with localStorage and sessionStorage kept per page per visitor and surviving new versions while cookies, IndexedDB and service workers are blocked, and ?query and #hash syncing with the address bar, which makes it a place to read a report and filter a table rather than a host for an authenticated app. AgentKeyGate at https://{host}/mcp i